first_experiment:
Build the world generator with 3 primitive families: orbit-symmetry, latent clean source, and hidden segmentation or deterministic channel. Sample candidates until there are 6 NEAR, 6 ALIEN and 4 NULL worlds (about 25 candidates; each costs 10 oracle-validation runs plus about 24 library-baseline runs).

Arms:
(a) bare Opus 5.5 in Claude Code headless;
(b) engine-v0 (the pilot's noisy and ask generators, plus dedupe and a critic/fixer), total tokens matched to (a);
(c) an oracle-hint arm, told the structure family, on 4 worlds only (upper bound).

Each arm gets 20 proxy runs per world, and every submitted patch is re-evaluated by the frozen evaluator on 5 fresh seeds. That is about 2,000 tiny runs in total (≈$25-160 GPU depending on per-run cost) plus about 36 agent campaigns × ≈$6 (≈$220), plus ≈$40 to build the generator. Total ≈$290-420.

Pre-stated success threshold for the instrument (all four must hold):
(1) >=12 of 16 worlds pass the acceptance criteria within the candidate budget;
(2) dynamic range: oracle-hint HR minus bare-Opus HR >= 0.25 on alien worlds;
(3) difficulty knob: bare-Opus HR on near worlds minus bare-Opus HR on alien worlds >= 0.20;
(4) the frozen e-value evaluator admits a gain on <=1 of 12 null-world submissions.

Kill or redesign if bare Opus reaches HR >= 0.8 on alien worlds (ceiling: cannot measure progress), if the oracle-hint arm stays below HR 0.3 (broken worlds), or if alien worlds cannot be constructed (the library always recovers >20%).

The engine-v0 vs bare difference on alien HR is reported with a bootstrap CI over worlds, but it is not a claim at n=6.

evaluation:
Execution only:
- HR measured by a frozen evaluator on hidden held-out data with fresh seeds;
- programmatic, exact verification of the claimed structure against the secret;
- null-world false-discovery rate under an anytime-valid e-value gate;
- runs and measured joules per discovery.

No LLM judges anywhere.

External validity is tested, not assumed. Across at least 5 harness variants, the Spearman correlation between alien-world HR and realized proxy gains on real post-cutoff tasks (from the CHSP proposal) must reach >=0.6 before PMW may be used as an optimization target for the harness.

mechanism:
1) Stream. Pull arXiv cs.LG/cs.CL papers whose first version is dated after the generator's training cutoff (from 2026-07-01 for Opus 5.5, whose cutoff is about June 2026), plus modded-nanogpt and nanochat PRs merged after the cutoff. Keep items that claim data, compute or energy efficiency for LM pretraining and can be implemented as a <=200-line patch at <=20M parameters (Haiku classifier plus human spot-check). Sample at random from the eligible set, never curate.
2) Card distillation. A separate call writes a results-stripped card: mechanism, patch target, knobs to tune, no reported numbers. A human checks fidelity on 20% of cards.
3) AI arms. Engine and bare Opus 5.5 get the same task (beat the tuned multi-epoch baseline at a fixed unique-token budget) and matched generation tokens. Their knowledge base is a frozen snapshot dated at the cutoff. There is no live search, because date filters leak.
4) Blind pool. All cards, human and AI, are canonicalized into one template by a different call, so implementers cannot infer the source.
5) Frozen critic ledger. Before any execution, record every critic's prediction: Opus absolute rubric (2 passes), a canonicalized Bradley-Terry pairwise tournament, and later the trained outcome predictor. SHA-256 the ledger and commit the hash to git.
6) Execution. Each card gets 3 independent implementations in fresh Claude Code sessions. Each implementation gets an equal tuning budget of 2 configs × 3 paired seeds on the proxy, and NVML energy is logged. Equal tuning budgets remove the 'known recipe vs untuned novelty' optimization gap.
7) Metrics:
- idea-level effect = mean log effective-data multiplier across implementations;
- idea ICC;
- arm-level probability of superiority P(AI idea > random contemporaneous human idea) with bootstrap CI;
- share of each arm in the pooled top decile;
- fraction beating 1.11x;
- cost and joules per idea that beats 1.11x;
- prospective AUC and calibration of every frozen critic;
- replication rate of recent human claims at proxy scale (a byproduct).
8) Recall discontinuity. Generate 100 cheap bare-Opus ideas. Compare their exact-mechanism match rate to a pre-cutoff human card set (Mar-May 2026) against a post-cutoff set (Jul-Sep 2026), using embedding nearest-neighbour plus a human check. A jump at the cutoff measures how much apparent quality is recall.
9) Labels. Every batch adds contamination-free, multi-implementation outcome labels. These train a small open outcome predictor (the Wen/Mule recipe), which is then tested prospectively on the next batch.

why_novel:
Earlier work uses post-cutoff human output as match targets (HindSight, Bolton & Torr, NanoGPT-Bench) or as published-number labels (Wen, Mule). Both reward conformity: a correct idea nobody published scores zero. CHSP instead runs the natural, uncurated human stream as a distributional control arm through the same automated executor, blind to source, with equal implementation effort and equal tuning budget. Good but unmatched AI ideas therefore get full credit, and generator quality comes out in the interpretable unit 'probability of beating this month's human research'. It also combines what no existing protocol has together:
- idea-level estimates from >=3 independent implementations, which controls the implementation lottery;
- hashed, pre-registered critic predictions, so every batch doubles as a prospective and contamination-free test set and training set for outcome predictors (Wen's prospective set had n=33, with a CI that includes chance);
- a recall-discontinuity estimate at the model's cutoff;
- continuous refresh, so it never saturates.

Si et al. is the nearest design, but it used expert humans at about 100 h per idea on commissioned ideas, a one-off.

mechanism:
1) Pool. Generate N ≈ 600-2,000 efficiency ideas from diverse generators (clean, ask-for-novelty, noisy persona, analogical-reasoning prompt, knowledge-base seeds) and convert them to canonical cards.
2) Evaluator panel, cheap and scoring every idea:
- E1 Opus absolute rubric (2 passes);
- E2 Bradley-Terry pairwise tournament on canonicalized, position-swapped cards;
- E4 small open outcome predictor fine-tuned on executed labels (from CHSP; added in later rounds);
- E5 low-fidelity execution (1 seed, 1/4 tokens; later rounds, since it needs implementation);
- E6 HindSight-style time-split match: SPECTER2 similarity to post-cutoff arXiv efficiency abstracts;
- E7 embedding distance to the knowledge base (a novelty signal).
3) Stratified execution. Stratify the pool jointly on E1 tertile × E2 tertile × E6 match/no-match. Sample about 60 ideas with known inclusion probabilities that oversample upper tails. Each sampled idea gets 2 independent implementations × 2 tuning configs × 3 paired seeds on the proxy.
4) Selection-validity curves. For each evaluator E and pressure q ∈ {1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/16}, estimate V_E(q) = E[realized log-multiplier | E in top q] minus the pool mean. Use Horvitz-Thompson weighting combined with prediction-powered inference (Angelopoulos et al., arXiv 2301.09633): all evaluators' scores act as cheap predictions and the executed subset gives valid CIs. One executed sample then serves every evaluator at once.
5) Presentation Goodhart, which costs nothing to execute. For 30 executed ideas, an optimizer agent rewrites each card over 4 rounds to maximize E1 and E2. A diff-checker locks the executable patch byte-for-byte, so any score change is pure gameability. Record Δscore and the share of ideas crossing the top-1/8 threshold.
6) Substance Goodhart (phase 2). Let the optimizer change the idea itself over k rounds against E, execute rounds 0, 3, 6 and 9, and plot realized gain against the E trajectory (a Gao-style overoptimization curve). k* is the round at which realized gain peaks.
7) False-zero audit. Report the share of executed ideas beating 1.11x that have E6 = 0 (no future-paper match).
8) Outputs per evaluator: curve with CI, gameability, k*, and a hill-climbable yes/no. Harness optimizers such as GEPA, tournaments or a UCB funnel may use E only when it passes, and only up to k*.
9) Re-anchor every quarter as generators change (the Red Queen principle).
